lesson: 139
title: Cuts and pieces, posts and gaps
level: primary-2
stars: 2
explain: To cut a log into 5 pieces you need only 4 cuts: the last piece needs no cut of its own.
explain: In the same way, 5 posts in a row have 4 gaps between them, and a clock striking 5 times has 4 pauses.
explain: So with 5 pieces and 3 minutes a cut, the sawing takes (5 − 1) × 3 = 12 minutes.
vars: a=name n=3..10 t=2..12
answer: (n - 1) * t
exercise: {a} saws a log into {n} pieces. Each cut takes {t} minutes. How many minutes does {a.he} spend sawing?
exercise: {n} posts stand in a row, {t} metres apart. How far is it from the first post to the last?
exercise: {a} cuts a ribbon into {n} pieces. Every cut takes {t} seconds. How many seconds does the cutting take?
exercise: {n} trees grow in a row along a path, with {t} metres between neighbours. How long is the row from the first tree to the last?
exercise: A clock strikes {n} times. The strikes are {t} seconds apart. How many seconds pass from the first strike to the last?
exercise: {a} lives on the {n.ordwords} floor. There are {t} steps from one floor to the next. How many steps does {a} climb from the first floor to {a.his} floor?
test: How many minutes does it take to cut a pole into {n} parts, if one cut takes {t} minutes?
test: {n} children stand in a line, each {t} metres from the next one. How many metres is it from the first child to the last?
test: To make {n} slices of a long loaf, {a} cuts it one cut at a time, {t} seconds a cut. How long does it take, in seconds?
test: A lift takes {t} seconds to go up one floor. How many seconds does it take to ride from the first floor to the {n.ordwords}?
exercise: How many minutes does {a} spend sawing, if {a.he} cuts a log into {n} pieces and each cut takes {t} minutes?
exercise: {n} pieces, {t} minutes per cut. How many minutes of cutting?
exercise: Along a road in Kansas stand {n} fence posts, each {t} metres from the next. How far is it from the first post to the last?
exercise: {a} chops a carrot into {n.words} pieces. Every cut takes {t.words} seconds. How many seconds does the chopping take?
exercise: There are {n} lamp posts in a line on a street in Edinburgh, with {t} metres between each pair of neighbours. What is the distance from the first lamp post to the last?
exercise: A church bell in Quebec rings {n} times, with {t} seconds between rings. How many seconds pass from the first ring to the last?
exercise: {a} cuts a plank into {n} equal parts. One cut takes {t} minutes. How long does all the cutting take, in minutes?
exercise: {n} flags hang on a string, {t} metres apart. How long is the string between the first flag and the last?
test: A baker in Perth slices a cake into {n} strips, taking {t} seconds for each cut. How many seconds does it take?
test: How far apart are the first and the last of {n} buoys in a line on a lake in Ontario, if the buoys are {t} metres apart?
test: {a} hears a clock strike {n} times. Between two strikes there are {t} seconds. How long, in seconds, from the first strike to the last?
test: {n} parts of a pipe come from cutting it, and every cut lasts {t} minutes. Find the total cutting time in minutes.
test: In a queue in Auckland, {n.words} children stand {t.words} metres apart. What distance separates the first child from the last?
test: The time for one cut is {t} minutes. A worker in Maine turns a log into {n} pieces. How many minutes of work is that?
exercise: {a} saws a log into {n} bits, each cut {t} mins. how long all the sawing
exercise: {n+2} posts — no sorry, {n} posts in a row, {t} m apart. first to last how far
exercise: my brother says cutting a ribbon into {n} pieces, {t} seconds a cut, is {n*t} seconds. nah, whats the right answer
exercise: {a} cuts a ribbon into {n} pieces, ribbon cost 4 dollars, each cut takes {t} seconds. how many seconds cutting
test: {n} trees in a line, {t} m between each two. how far first tree to last
test: chop a plank in {n} bits, {t} min per cut. mins altogether?
test: {a} reckons {n} posts {t} metres apart is {(n+1)*t} metres end to end. wrong yeah? how far really
exam: It is given that a log is divided by sawing into {n} pieces and that each cut occupies {t} minutes. Determine the total time, in minutes, devoted to sawing.
exam: Whereas {n} posts are erected in a row at intervals of {t} metres, ascertain the distance in metres between the first post and the last.
exam: A ribbon is to be cut into {n} pieces, each cut requiring {t} seconds. State the total duration of the cutting in seconds.
exam: Compute the length, in metres, of a fence line along which {n} posts are set {t} metres apart, measured from the first post to the last.
exercise: Grandpa's old apple tree gave up the ghost, so {a} saws its trunk into {n} logs. Each cut takes {t} minutes. How many minutes does {a.he} spend sawing?
exercise: The old fence by the road bit the dust in a storm. Now {n} new posts stand in a row, {t} m apart. How far is it from the first post to the last?
test: {a}'s stretching the truth again, says sawing a log into {n} bits at {t} min a cut took {n*t} min. load of rubbish, is {a.he} thick or what. how long did it really take
